<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="DefinitionFunction" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Definition of a Function</title>
    <p>
        In this section, we will definine a function and learn how to identify the
        input and output variables of graphs, tables, diagrams, and equations.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="function-definition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A <term>function</term> is a relationship between two variables where every <term>input</term> 
                corresponds to exactly one <term>output</term>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
  Depending on the situation, we may refer to the relationship between a function and its inputs and outputs as a
  <term>mapping</term>, a <term>correspondence</term>, or a <term>rule</term>. We also have several ways to represent
  functions. We can use a <term>diagram</term>, a <term>table</term>, a <term>graph</term>, or an <term>equation</term>.
</p>

<definition xml:id="function-notation-definition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The representation of a function <m>y=f(x)</m> is called <term>function notation</term>.
                The letter <m>f</m> is the name of the function, and the letter <m>x</m> is the input variable, and the
                letter <m>y</m> is the output variable. We say that <m>y</m> is a function of <m>x</m>.
            </p>

      <figure xml:id="function-notation-figure">
     <image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="function-notation-prefigure">
        <description>Function Notation</description>
            <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="(-10, -100, -50, -100)">
  <coordinates bbox="(-2,-2,2,2)">
     <label anchor="(0,0)" alignment="center">
      <m>y=f(x)</m>
     </label>
     <path start="(-1,.5)" stroke="blue" arrows="1">
      <quadratic-bezier controls="((-.4,.5),(-.4,.1))"/>
    </path>
    <label anchor="(-1.6,.45)" alignment="center" color="blue">
      Output
     </label>
     <path start="(-.5,-0.5)" stroke="red" arrows="1">
      <quadratic-bezier controls="((.05,-.5),(.05,-.2))"/>
      </path>
      <label anchor="(-1.9,-.55)" alignment="west" color="red">
      Function Name
     </label>
     <path start="(.7,-0.5)" stroke="green" arrows="1">
      <quadratic-bezier controls="((.3,-.5),(.3,-.2))"/>
      </path>
      <label anchor="(.7,-.55)" alignment="east" color="green">
      Input
     </label>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure> 
 </image>
</figure>
        </statement>
    </definition>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-Is-it-a-Function-Diagrams">
      <title>Is it a Function - Diagrams
      </title>
      
      <p>
      Below, in <xref ref="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure1" text="type-global"/> and <xref ref="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure2" text="type-global"/> we have two examples of relationships which <em>are functions</em>. Notice that each input A, B, and C is mapped to exactly one output value. Two inputs can share the same output value, but each input must have a unique output value.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example; in the first diagram, <xref ref="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure1" text="type-global"/>, each input is going to a different output value.
      In the second diagram, <xref ref="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure2" text="type-global"/>, inputs B and C are both going to 
      the same output value of Pear, but each input is still going to a single output value.
    </p>
    <p> Visually, a diagram which represents a function will always have a single arrow coming out of each input variable.
    </p>

<sidebyside>   
<figure xml:id="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure1">
     <image width="60%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="Bubble-Diagram-prefigure1">
        <title>Bubble Diagram</title>
        <description>Bubble diagram of a function.</description>
     <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
  <definition> mid=2 </definition>
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
  <definition> mid=2 </definition> 
  <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
    <label anchor="(-mid,3.4)" alignment="center">
      Input
     </label>
    <ellipse center="(-mid,0)" axes="(1.5,3)" stroke="black"/>
    <label anchor="(mid,3.4)" alignment="center">
      Output
     </label>
    <ellipse center="(mid,0)" axes="(1.5,3)" stroke="black"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,0), (mid-.6,top))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,top), (mid-.6,-top))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,-top), (mid-.5,0))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,top)" alignment="center">
      Apple
    </label>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,0)" alignment="center">
      Pear
    </label>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,-top-.1)" alignment="center">
      Grape
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,top)" alignment="center">
      A
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,0)" alignment="center">
      B
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,-top)" alignment="center">
      C
    </label>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure> 
 </image>
</figure>


<figure xml:id="Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure2">
     <image width="60%">
    <caption>This is a Function</caption>
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="Bubble-Diagram-prefigure2">
        <title>Bubble Diagram</title>
        <description>Bubble diagram of a function.</description>
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
  <definition> mid=2 </definition>
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
  <definition> mid=2 </definition> 
  <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
    <label anchor="(-mid,3.4)" alignment="center">
      Input
     </label>
     <ellipse center="(-mid,0)" axes="(1.5,3)" stroke="black"/>
     <label anchor="(mid,3.4)" alignment="center">
      Output
     </label>
     <ellipse center="(mid,0)" axes="(1.5,3)" stroke="black"/>
     <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,0), (mid-.7,0))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
     <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,top), (mid-.6,-top))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
     <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,-top), (mid-.6,-0.1))" stroke="blue" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
     <label anchor="(mid-.4,top)" alignment="center">
      Apple
     </label>
     <label anchor="(mid-.4,0)" alignment="center">
      Pear
     </label>
     <label anchor="(mid-.4,-top-.1)" alignment="center">
      Grape
     </label>
     <label anchor="(-mid-.2,top)" alignment="center">
      A
     </label>
     <label anchor="(-mid-.2,0)" alignment="center">
      B
     </label>
     <label anchor="(-mid-.2,-top)" alignment="center">
      C
     </label>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure> 
 </image>
</figure>
</sidebyside> 

<p>
  In contrast, the diagrams below are both examples of relationships that are <em>not functions</em>. There is an input which has two different outputs in each diagram.
</p>
<p>
  In the first diagram, <xref ref="not-Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure1" text="type-global"/>, input B is going to both output values of Apple and Pear.
  In the second diagram, <xref ref="not-Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure2" text="type-global"/>, input A is going to both output values of Apple and Grape.
</p>

<sidebyside>   
<figure xml:id="not-Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure1">
     <image width="60%">
    <caption></caption>
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="not-Bubble-Diagram-prefigure1">
        <title>Bubble Diagram</title>
        <description>Bubble diagram of a function.</description>
     <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
  <definition> mid=2 </definition>
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
  <definition> mid=2 </definition> 
  <coordinates bbox="(-4,-4,4,4)">
    <label anchor="(-mid,3.4)" alignment="center">
      Input
     </label>
    <ellipse center="(-mid,0)" axes="(1.5,3)" stroke="black"/>
    <label anchor="(mid,3.4)" alignment="center">
      Output
     </label>
    <ellipse center="(mid,0)" axes="(1.5,3)" stroke="black"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,0), (mid-.6,top))" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,0), (mid-.6,0))" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,top), (mid-.6,-top))" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <line endpoints="((-mid+.2,-top), (mid-.5,0))" stroke="red" thickness="2" arrows="1"/>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,top)" alignment="center">
      Apple
    </label>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,0)" alignment="center">
      Pear
    </label>
    <label anchor="(mid-.4,-top-.1)" alignment="center">
      Grape
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,top)" alignment="center">
      A
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,0)" alignment="center">
      B
    </label>
    <label anchor="(-mid-.2,-top)" alignment="center">
      C
    </label>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure> 
 </image>
</figure>


<figure xml:id="not-Func-Bubble-Diagram-figure2">
     <image width="60%">
    <caption>This is a Function</caption>
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="not-Func-Bubble-Diagram-prefigure2">
        <title>Bubble Diagram</title>
        <description>Bubble diagram of a function.</description>
<diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> top=1.75 </definition>
